# TLS in front, because the phone will not talk to it otherwise.
#
# iOS App Transport Security refuses plain HTTP from an app, so the
# registration call needs a certificate — and a certificate needs a name. Caddy
# gets one from Let's Encrypt on first start and renews it without being asked.
#
# push.cubechat.tech is the real name and the one the app asks for first. It
# needs one A record pointing here; the apex stays on GitHub Pages for the
# marketing site and is not touched.
#
# The sslip.io block stays until no shipped build is still asking for it. It
# was the way in while cubechat.qpon sat undelegated in the .qpon registry —
# sslip.io resolves any `a-b-c-d.sslip.io` to the address written in the name —
# and that same trick is what DNS rebinding protection blocks, which is the
# reason it is no longer the first choice. See `push_registration.dart`.
#
# cubechat.qpon is kept out: it has never resolved, so Caddy would fail the
# ACME challenge for it on every start.

push.cubechat.tech {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}

209-38-225-225.sslip.io {
	reverse_proxy 127.0.0.1:8080
}
